{
  "configuration_id": "config_008",
  "reason": "Evidence: We have only one completed experiment (config_015: image_only, singleprofile, resnet50 → acc 0.208, F1 0.172), which establishes a weak baseline. The critical uncertainty is whether adding the RNA modality provides any benefit over image-only features at all. Question: config_008 (multimodal, singleprofile, pca_n2000, resnet50, no aggregation, concat) keeps the image encoder (resnet50) and profile setting identical to the baseline, adds the simplest RNA representation (PCA, no learned parameters), and uses the simplest fusion (concatenation). This isolates the effect of adding RNA information with minimal confounding. Why this is the best next step: With a budget-limited search and only one data point, the highest-information experiment is to determine whether the multimodal direction is viable at all. If config_008 substantially outperforms config_015, we gain confidence to invest in richer RNA encoders, multiprofile settings, and learned aggregation/fusion. If it does not, we avoid wasting budget on increasingly complex multimodal variants and can redirect toward image-encoder upgrades or RNA-only baselines. Changing only the modality (and using the simplest possible RNA pipeline) makes the comparison clean and interpretable."
}

